3.m:
Hither let the sacrifice pass over from that one to me, / he who is the domestic priest of the ṛṣis.
Having made him godless and vigorless, let Viṣkandha be cast down upon him who hates us;
let the body, the sacrifice-impurity, the debt settle upon him who hates us.
O sacrifice, with that brilliance of the sacrifice, pass over to me;
by the heat of the sacrifice, I, so-and-so, summon to you the brahmins, the officiating priests, the gods.
What has been cooked by the offering I summon to you, I, so-and-so;
I wrest away your good deed, your offspring, your cattle.
Directions, kindle-verses, butter-pourings, portions of ghee, the call and counter-call I hear away from you;
fore-offerings, after-offerings, the Sviṣṭakṛt, the iḍā, the blessings, heaven I wrest away.
With Agni, Indra, Soma, Sarasvatī, Viṣṇu, with the deities,
with the offering- and invitatory-verses I summon to you, I, so-and-so; I take away your sacrifice that has been consecrated with vaṣaṭ.
Praise-hymn, recitation, response, draught, iḍā, blessings, heaven I wrest away;
the offerings to the wives of the gods I summon to you, I, so-and-so; I take away your Samiṣṭayajus.
Cattle, pressed soma, cake-offerings, soma-pressings, and the sacrifice,
the gods with Indra, I summon to you, I, so-and-so, those whose face is Agni, who possess soma, and all [the gods] who are there.

Let houses come to me, let offspring come to me, let the sacrifice enter me full of vigor;
let the divine, worshipful waters enter me, let the abundance of a thousand not abandon me.
Let draughts come to me, let the shining recitation, the praise-hymn and recitation concordant enter me;
let the Ādityas, the Rudras, the Vasus be my fellow-sitters, let the abundance of a thousand not abandon me.
Let the Agniṣṭoma enter me, and the Ukthya, let the Atirātra enter me, [and the rite] touching the night;
let the well-offered over-night draughts enter me, let the abundance of a thousand not abandon me.

Through Agni, heat followed;
through speech, the sacred formulation;
through the jewel, forms;
through gold, splendor;
through the waters, the earth;
through the wind, the intermediate space;
through the sun, heaven;
through the moon, the lunar mansions;
through Yama, the fathers;
through the king, humans;
through fruit, river-dwelling creatures;
through the python, serpents;
through the tiger, wild animals;
through the falcon, winged creatures;
through rice, food;
through barley, plants;
through the udumbara, vigor;
through the banyan, trees;
through the stallion, horses;
through the bull, cows;
through the ram, sheep;
through the he-goat, goats;
through the Gāyatrī, the meters;
through the Trivṛt, the praise-songs;
through the Rathantara, the chants;
through the brahmin, speech.

4.m:
"Who travels alone? Who is born again?
What is the remedy for cold? What is the great receptacle?"
"The sun travels alone; the moon is born again;
fire is the remedy for cold; the earth is the great receptacle."
"What was the first thought? What was the great bird?
What was the slippery one? What was the tawny one?"
"Heaven was the first thought; the horse was the great bird;
the ewe was the slippery one; the night was the tawny one."
"I ask you about the furthest limit of the earth; I ask you about the navel of the world;
I ask you about the seed of the vigorous horse; I ask you about the highest realm of speech."
"They say the sacrificial altar is the furthest limit of the earth; they say the sacrifice is the navel of the world;
they say soma is the seed of the vigorous horse; the Brahman itself is the highest realm of speech."

"O dear mother, little mother, mother! No one leads me;
the wretched horse sleeps, O fortunate one, O dweller of Kāmpīla!"
Cover yourselves together in the heavenly world!
"I drive in the placer of the seed;
do you drive in the placer of the seed."
Let both of us stretch out the four legs together;
may the bull, the placer of seed, deposit the seed!
"Set the anus above the two thighs, drive in the anointing, the high-anointing!"
He who is the enjoyment of life for women, who runs into their aperture,
dear to women, secret, who struck against
their anus in the dark mark.
"O dear mother, little mother, mother! No one leads me;
the wretched horse sleeps!"
"Raise her up high like a load of bamboo on the mountain;
then let her middle thrive, as if winnowing in a cold wind!"
"O mother!" is spoken.
"When the female deer eats the barley, one does not consider the beast well-nourished;
when the Śūdrā woman is the lover of an Arya, she does not seek wealth for prosperity."
"O mother!" is spoken.
"This little bird here flits about crying 'ahalam';
when the penis is thrust into the vulva, the receiver swallows it greedily."
"O mother!" is spoken.
"Both your mother and your father climb to the top of the tree;
'I am thrusting it forward!' says your father, as he brandishes his fist in the vulva."
"I have praised Dadhikrāvan..."
"Waters, you are indeed..." are three [verses].

5.m:
Agni was the sacrificial animal;
with him he sacrificed;
he won this world in which Agni is;
that is your world;
you will win it.
Now sniff [it].
Vāyu was the sacrificial animal;
with him he sacrificed;
he won this world in which Vāyu is;
that is your world;
you will win it.
Now sniff [it].
Āditya was the sacrificial animal;
with him he sacrificed;
he won this world in which Āditya is;
that is your world; you will win it.
Now sniff [it].

Heaven is your back;
the earth is your seat;
the intermediate space is your self;
the ocean is your womb;
the sun is your eye;
the wind is your breath;
the moon is your ear;
the quarters are your feet;
the intermediate quarters are your animals;
the lunar mansions are your form;
the months and the half-months are your joints;
the seasons are your limbs;
the year is your majesty.

To Agni belonging to the Gāyatrī, threefold, associated with the Rathantara, vernal, [a cake] on eight potsherds;
to Indra belonging to the Triṣṭubh, fifteenfold, associated with the Bṛhat, aestival, [a cake] on eleven potsherds;
to the All-Gods belonging to the Jagatī, seventeenfold, associated with the Vairūpa, rainy, [a cake] on twelve potsherds;
to Mitra and Varuṇa belonging to the Anuṣṭubh, twenty-one-fold, associated with the Vairāja, autumnal, curds;
to Bṛhaspati belonging to the Paṅkti, thrice-nine-fold, associated with the Śākvara, wintry, a mess of boiled rice;
to Savitṛ belonging to the Aticchandas, thirty-three-fold, associated with the Raivata, dewy, [a cake] on twelve potsherds;
to Aditi, wife of Viṣṇu, a mess of boiled rice;
to Agni Vaiśvānara [a cake] on twelve potsherds;
to Anumati a mess of boiled rice;
to Ka [a cake] on one potsherd.

The prize-winner has stepped onto the earth;
Agni as companion has the prize-winning courser made.
The prize-winner has stepped onto the intermediate space;
Vāyu as companion has the prize-winning courser made.
Onto heaven has the prize-winner stepped;
Sūrya as companion has the prize-winning courser made.
Life is your yoker, O prize-winner;
I take hold of you from behind;
bring me safely across!
Vāyu is your yoker, O prize-winner;
I take hold of you from behind;
bring me safely across!
Āditya is your yoker, O prize-winner;
I take hold of you from behind;
bring me safely across!
You are the sustainer of out-breath;
strengthen my out-breath!
You are the sustainer of diffused breath;
strengthen my diffused breath!
You are the sustainer of in-breath;
strengthen my in-breath!
You are sight;
give me sight!
You are hearing;
place hearing in me!
You are life;
give me life!
May I attain a full lifespan!

May the seed be productive;
Parjanya the bringer of rain;
the corn ripened;
the plants rich in berries;
this [earth] good to walk upon;
Agni easy to approach;
the intermediate space well-governed;
the purifying [wind] blowing gently;
heaven easy to attend;
that one shining auspiciously;
the day and night as before;
the half-months fifteen-dayed;
the months thirty-dayed;
the seasons ordered;
the year peaceful!

To Agni [a cake] on eight potsherds;
to Soma a mess of boiled rice;
to Savitṛ [a cake] on eight potsherds;
to Sarasvatī a mess of boiled rice;
to Pūṣan a mess of boiled rice;
to Rudra a mess of boiled gavidhukā rice;
to Agni Vaiśvānara [a cake] on twelve potsherds.
If [the horse] should not return to the wild beast's lair:
to Agni deliverer from distress [a cake] on eight potsherds;
to Sūrya milk;
to Vāyu the portion of clarified butter.

To Agni deliverer from distress [a cake] on eight potsherds;
to Indra deliverer from distress [a cake] on eleven potsherds;
to Vāyu and Savitṛ, the deliverers from sin, a mess of boiled rice;
to Mitra and Varuṇa, the deliverers from sin, curds;
to the Aśvins, the deliverers from sin, roasted grains;
to the Maruts, the deliverers from fault, [a cake] on seven potsherds;
to the All-Gods, the deliverers from fault, [a cake] on twelve potsherds;
to Anumati a mess of boiled rice;
to Agni Vaiśvānara [a cake] on twelve potsherds;
to Heaven and Earth, the deliverers from distress, [a cake] on two potsherds.

The fuel of the directions;
By what [hymn] for us...;
Who yokes today to the pole the cows of truth, the vigorous, wrathful, ill-disposed ones,
with arrows in their mouth, wounding the heart, giving delight? He who accomplishes their maintenance, he shall live!
Thus the fifth measured recitation.

6.m:
When you neighed...;
From afar I recognized your soul with my mind, the bird flying downward from heaven;
I saw your head fluttering on dustless paths, easy to traverse, as a winged creature.
There I saw your highest form, striving to conquer food in the place of the cow;
when a mortal enjoyed your service, then indeed, greediest, did you awaken the plants.
After you the chariot, after you the youth, O courser; after you the cows, after you the charm of maidens;
after you the troops went for your friendship; the gods measured out your heroic might.
Golden-horned, of iron were his feet; swift as thought, Indra was lower than he;
the gods indeed came to his oblation-eating, who was the first to mount the courser.
Broad-rumped, slender-waisted, vigorous, heavenly race-horses;
like geese they line up in rows, when the horses reached the heavenly course.
Your body is winged, O courser; your spirit rushes like the wind;
your horns, scattered in many places, roam glittering in the wilderness.
The prize-winning courser has approached the slaughter-place, meditating with mind directed to the gods;
the goat is led before him, his navel; behind him go the wise poets, the singers.
The courser has approached the highest assembly, toward father and mother;
may you go today most welcome to the gods; then ask for desirable things for the patron!

7.m:
For King Indra, a boar;
for King Varuṇa, a black [animal];
for King Yama, a blackbuck;
for King Ṛṣabha, a gayal;
for King Tiger, a gaur;
for the King of Men, a monkey;
for the swift falcon, a quail;
for the nilgai, a worm;
for King Soma, an antelope;
for the Indus, a dolphin;
for the Himavat, an elephant.

The mayu is Prajāpati's;
the owl, the halikṣṇa, the wildcat are Dhātṛ's;
for Sarasvatī, a white thrush that speaks like a man;
for Sarasvant, a white parrot that speaks like a man;
the wild goat, the mongoose, the dung-beetle are Pūṣan's;
for Speech, a heron.

For the Son of Waters, a large fish;
the crocodile, the alligator, the crab are Kūvara's;
for Speech, a paiṅgarāja-bird;
for Bhaga, a kuṣītaka-bird;
the duck, the vāhasa, the woodpecker are Vāyu's;
for the quarters, a cakravāka.

For Strength, a python;
the mouse, the hedgehog, the lizard are Mitra's;
for Death, a black snake;
for Wrath, a viper;
the jar-born serpent, the lotus-dweller, the red snake are Tvaṣṭṛ's;
for the Echo, a vāhasa.

A human-beast for the Moon;
the monitor lizard, the black snake, the tree-striker are the Trees';
a black doe for the Day;
a black [animal] for the Night;
the piga, the kṣviṅkā, the blue-headed [bird] are Aryaman's;
for Dhātṛ, a cock.

For the Sun, a crane;
the blackbuck, the peacock, the falcon are the Gandharvas';
for the Vasus, a francolin;
for the Rudras, a partridge;
the red deer, the kuṇḍṛṇācī, the lizard are the Apsarases';
for the Forest, a wild goat.

A dappled [beast] belongs to the All-gods;
the bidva, the antelope, the weasel are Anumati's;
the cuckoo belongs to the Half-months;
for the Months, a tortoise;
the kuvayi, the cock, the gallinule are Sinīvālī's;
for Bṛhaspati, a śimyuṭa.

The dung-beetle belongs to the Earth;
the badger, the weasel, the manthīlava are the Fathers';
of the Seasons, the hedgehog;
for the Year, an owl;
the dove, the owl, the hare are Nirṛti's;
a cock is Savitṛ's.

A ruru-deer belongs to Rudra;
the chameleon, the bird, the pippakā are Śaravyā's;
a gazelle is the Maruts';
for Brahman, a horned owl;
the hyena, the black four-eyed dog, the donkey are the other people's;
for Agni, a woodpecker.

The alaja belongs to the Atmosphere;
the otter, the diver, the swimming bird are the Waters';
for Aditi, a white-winged goose;
for Indrāṇī, a bat;
the vulture, the white-rumped [vulture], the rhinoceros are Aditi's;
for Heaven and Earth, a porcupine.

The eagle is Parjanya's;
the goose, the wolf, the wildcat are Indra's;
of the Waters, the otter;
of Aryaman, the jackal;
the lion, the mongoose, the tiger are Great Indra's;
for Desire, the wild ass.
Thus the seventh [section], the recitation of Indra.

8.m:
Belonging to Agni is a black-necked [goat];
for Sarasvatī, a ewe;
a brown one belongs to Soma;
Pūṣan's is a dark-grey one;
white-backed is Bṛhaspati's;
variegated is the All-gods';
Indra's is a reddish one;
the Maruts' is a piebald one;
Indra and Agni's is a joined one;
Savitṛ's is black-bellied;
Varuṇa's is a ram.

A horse, a hornless goat, a nilgai are Prajāpati's;
two black-necked ones belong to Agni;
two shaggy-thighed ones to Tvaṣṭṛ;
two white-backed ones to Bṛhaspati;
for Dhātṛ, one with a dappled belly;
Sūrya's is a white ram.

For Agni with the frontline, a red-streaked draft-ox;
two black-bellied ones for Savitṛ;
two with silver navels for Pūṣan;
two tawny, hornless ones for the All-gods;
the Maruts' is a piebald one;
Agni's is a black he-goat;
for Sarasvatī, a ewe;
Varuṇa's is a black ram with one white foot.
Thus the eighth [section], the recitation of the ram.

9.m:
A red one, a smoky-red one, a jujube-red one are Prajāpati's;
a brown one, a reddish-brown one, a parrot-brown one are Rudra's;
a white one, a white-eyed one, a white-necked one belong to the Fathers as deities;
three black barren cows are Varuṇa's;
three white barren cows are Sūrya's;
belonging to Mitra and Bṛhaspati are smoky-spotted, hornless ones.

A speckled one, a crosswise-speckled one, an upward-speckled one are the Maruts';
a light-colored one, a red-wooled one, a white one are Sarasvatī's;
a dappled one, a small-dappled one, a coarse-dappled one are the All-gods';
three dark-grey barren cows are Pūṣan's;
three red barren cows are Mitra's;
belonging to Indra and Bṛhaspati are red-spotted, hornless ones.

A white-forelegged one, one white-forelegged on one side, one white-forelegged all around are Indra and Vāyu's;
a white-groined one, one white-groined on one side, one white-groined all around are Mitra and Varuṇa's;
a pure-tailed one, a completely pure-tailed one, a jewel-tailed one are the Aśvins';
three variegated barren cows are the All-gods';
three falcon-colored ones are Parameṣṭhin's;
belonging to Soma and Pūṣan are dark-spotted, hornless ones.

A raised one, a bull, a dwarf are Indra and Varuṇa's;
a white-humped one, a white-backed one, a white-rumped one are Indra and Bṛhaspati's;
a white-footed one, a white-lipped one, a white-browed one are Indra and Viṣṇu's;
three leprous-spotted barren cows are Viśvakarman's;
three with dappled bellies are Dhātṛ's; Pūṣan's are white-spotted, hornless ones.

Three crop-eared ones are Yama's;
three white-thighed ones are Soma's;
for Agni the youngest, three mongoose-colored ones;
three three-year-old red cows belong to the Vasus;
three two-and-a-half-year-old reddish cows belong to the Rudras;
belonging to Soma and Indra are brown-spotted, hornless ones.

Three tail-less ones are Viṣṇu's;
three with hanging-down ears are for Viṣṇu the wide-striding;
three shaggy-faced ones are for Viṣṇu the wide-stepping;
three five-year-old cows are the Ādityas';
three three-year-old heifers are the Aṅgirases';
belonging to Agni and Indra are black-spotted, hornless ones.

For Indra the king, three white-backed ones;
for Indra the overlord, three white-humped ones;
for Indra the self-ruler, three white-rumped ones;
three two-year-old heifers are the Sādhyas';
three three-year-old heifers are the All-gods';
belonging to Indra and Viṣṇu are bos-gaurus-spotted, hornless ones.

For Aditi, three red-variegated ones;
for Indrāṇī, three black-variegated ones;
for Kuhū, three reddish-variegated ones;
three milk cows are for Rākā;
three draft-oxen are for Sinīvālī;
belonging to Agni and Viṣṇu are red-spotted, hornless ones.

Three tawny ones are Soma's;
for Soma the king, three dapple-bodied ones;
cloud-like ones are Parjanya's;
three dewlapped she-goats are for Indrāṇī;
three ewes are the Ādityas';
belonging to Heaven and Earth are variegated, hornless ones.

Varuṇa's are three black-spotted ones;
for Varuṇa the king, three red-spotted ones;
for Varuṇa the destroyer of enemies, three reddish-spotted ones;
three variegated ones are the All-gods';
three speckled ones belong to all the gods as deities;
belonging to Indra and Sūrya are dark-spotted, hornless ones.
Thus the ninth [section], the recitation of the red ones.

10.m:
For Soma the self-ruler, two cart-drawing draft-oxen;
for Indra and Agni, granters of vigor, two snout-nosed beasts;
for Indra and Agni, granters of strength, two plow-drawing sheep;
two milk cows belong to the Earth;
two mares for the quarters;
two milk cows belong to the Earth;
two human women for Virāj;
two milk cows belong to the Earth;
for Vāyu, two draft-oxen drawing a mount;
two black barren cows belong to Varuṇa;
two divine Arāḍi cows, two encircling bulls.

Eleven cattle victims are seized in the morning;
a piebald he-goat, a kikidīvi bird, and a vidīgaya bird, those two are Tvaṣṭṛ's;
nine white barren cows belonging to Sūrya become the subsequent victims;
those for Agni, Indra-Agni, and the Aśvins are seized at the broad sacrificial post.

Three tawny ones are of spring;
three dapple-bodied ones are of summer;
three dappled ones are of the rainy season;
three speckled ones are of autumn;
three speckled-thighed ones are of winter;
three smeared ones are of the cool season;
for the Year, ones with low chests.

Who cuts you up? Who carves you? Who stitches your limbs?
And who is your wise slaughterer?
Let the seasons, the slaughterers, carve your joint season by season;
by the nourishment of the year, let them stitch you with stitching acts.
Let the divine Adhvaryus cut you up and carve you;
joint by joint let them make stitchings, stitching your limbs.
Let the half-months, stitching, cut up your joints, [and so] the months;
let the Maruts, the days and nights, bring into order what is torn apart.
Let the earth with the intermediate space, let the wind heal your defect;
let heaven together with the constellations make your form, [and so] the Sādhyas.
May there be welfare to your upper limbs, welfare to your lower ones;
welfare to the bones, to the marrow, and may there be welfare to your body.
Thus the tenth [section], the recitation of Soma.

11.m:
Homage to Agni;
homage to the Gāyatrī;
homage to the Trivṛt;
homage to the Rathantara;
homage to spring;
homage to the eastern quarter;
homage to the out-breath;
homage to the Vasus.

Homage to Indra;
homage to the Triṣṭubh;
homage to the Pañcadaśa;
homage to the Bṛhat;
homage to summer;
homage to the southern quarter;
homage to the inter-breath;
homage to the Rudras.

Homage to the Maruts;
homage to the Jagatī;
homage to the Saptadaśa;
homage to the Vairūpa;
homage to the rainy season;
homage to the western quarter;
homage to the in-breath;
homage to the Ādityas.

Homage to the All-gods;
homage to the Anuṣṭubh;
homage to the Ekaviṃśa;
homage to the Vairāja;
homage to autumn;
homage to the northern quarter;
homage to the through-breath;
homage to the Moon.

Homage to Mitra and Varuṇa;
homage to the Paṅkti;
homage to the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa;
homage to the Śākvara and Raivata;
homage to winter and the cool season;
homage to the upward quarter;
homage to the up-breath;
homage to Viṣṇu.

Homage to the earth;
homage to Agni;
homage to the past;
homage to sight;
homage to the intermediate space;
homage to Vāyu;
homage to the future;
homage to hearing;
homage to heaven;
homage to the Sun;
homage to what is yet to come;
homage to the mind.
Thus ends the eleventh section: the formulas of homage.
